persistent/pubmed-meta-image-v2.jpg">     <!-- OpenSearch XML -->   <link rel="search" type="application/opensearchdescription+xml" href="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/persistent/opensearch.xml" title="PubMed search">    <!-- Disables severely broken elements when no JS -->   <noscript>     <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/a447896c-976f-490b-b86b-2668b65114a8/core/no-script.css">   </noscript>         <link rel="canonical" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=MECR%5Btiab%5D+AND+2021%5Bdp%5D&amp;format=pubmed&amp;size=50" />      </head> <body >       <main class="search-page" id="search-page">     <div class="search-results" id="search-results">                <pre class="search-results-chunk">PMID- 33401012\r OWN - NLM\r STAT- MEDLINE\r DCOM- 20211102\r LR  - 20221207\r IS  - 1872-8278 (Electronic)\r IS  - 1567-7249 (Linking)\r VI  - 57\r DP  - 2021 Mar\r TI  - Whole exome sequencing identifies a novel homozygous MECR mutation in a Chinese \r       patient with childhood-onset dystonia and basal ganglia abnormalities, without \r       optic atrophy.\r PG  - 222-229\r LID - S1567-7249(20)30245-2 [pii]\r LID - 10.1016/j.mito.2020.12.014 [doi]\r AB  - Childhood-onset dystonia with optic atrophy and basal ganglia abnormalities is an \r       extremely rare autosomal recessive mitochondrial disease caused by biallelic \r       mutations in MECR. Using whole-exome sequencing, we identified a novel homozygous \r       MECR mutation (c.910G &gt; T, p.Asp304Tyr) in a Chinese patient with childhood-onset \r       dystonia and basal ganglia abnormalities, without optic atrophy. With lipoic acid \r       treatment, the disease progression was under control, and neither visual \r       impairment nor optic atrophy was observed. To our knowledge, this is the first \r       study about MECR-related mitochondrial disease in a Chinese patient and the first \r       to report that supplementation with lipoic acid is a possible effective \r       therapeutic strategy for this disease.\r CI  - Copyright © 2021 The Authors. Published by Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.\r FAU - Liu, Zhimei\r AU  - Liu Z\r AD  - Department of Neurology, Beijing Children&#x27;s Hospital, Capital Medical University, \r       National Center for Children&#x27;s Health, Beijing 100045, China.\r FAU - Shimura, Masaru\r AU  - Shimura M\r AD  - Center for Medical Genetics and Department of Metabolism, Chiba Children&#x27;s \r       Hospital, Chiba 2660007, Japan.\r FAU - Zhang, Li\r AU  - Zhang L\r AD  - Center for Bioinformatics and Computational Biology, and the Institute of \r       Biomedical Sciences, School of Life Sciences, East China Normal University, \r       Shanghai 200241, China.\r FAU - Zhang, Weihua\r AU  - Zhang W\r AD  - Department of Neurology, Beijing Children&#x27;s Hospital, Capital Medical University, \r       National Center for Children&#x27;s Health, Beijing 100045, China.\r FAU - Wang, Jianing\r AU  - Wang J\r AD  - College of Life Sciences, Nankai University, Tianjin 300071, China.\r FAU - Ogawa-Tominaga, Minako\r AU  - Ogawa-Tominaga M\r AD  - Center for Medical Genetics and Department of Metabolism, Chiba Children&#x27;s \r       Hospital, Chiba 2660007, Japan.\r FAU - Wang, Junling\r AU  - Wang J\r AD  - Department of Neurology, Beijing Children&#x27;s Hospital, Capital Medical University, \r       National Center for Children&#x27;s Health, Beijing 100045, China.\r FAU - Wang, Xiaohui\r AU  - Wang X\r AD  - Department of Neurology, Beijing Children&#x27;s Hospital, Capital Medical University, \r       National Center for Children&#x27;s Health, Beijing 100045, China.\r FAU - Lv, Junlan\r AU  - Lv J\r AD  - Department of Neurology, Beijing Children&#x27;s Hospital, Capital Medical University, \r       National Center for Children&#x27;s Health, Beijing 100045, China.\r FAU - Shi, Wei\r AU  - Shi W\r AD  - Department of Ophthalmology, Beijing Children&#x27;s Hospital, Capital Medical \r       University, National Center for Children&#x27;s Health, Beijing 100045, China.\r FAU - Zhang, Victor Wei\r AU  - Zhang VW\r AD  - AmCare Genomics Lab, GuangZhou 510006, China.\r FAU - Murayama, Kei\r AU  - Murayama K\r AD  - Center for Medical Genetics and Department of Metabolism, Chiba Children&#x27;s \r       Hospital, Chiba 2660007, Japan. Electronic address: kmuraya@mri.biglobe.ne.jp.\r FAU - Fang, Fang\r AU  - Fang F\r AD  - Department of Neurology, Beijing Children&#x27;s Hospital, Capital Medical University, \r       National Center for Children&#x27;s Health, Beijing 100045, China. Electronic address: \r       13910150389@163.com.\r LA  - eng\r PT  - Journal Article\r PT  - Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov&#x27;t\r DEP - 20210102\r PL  - Netherlands\r TA  - Mitochondrion\r JT  - Mitochondrion\r JID - 100968751\r RN  - 73Y7P0K73Y (Thioctic Acid)\r RN  - EC 1.3.- (Oxidoreductases Acting on CH-CH Group Donors)\r RN  - EC 1.3.1.38 (trans-2-enoyl-CoA reductase (NADPH))\r SB  - IM\r MH  - Basal Ganglia/*abnormalities/drug effects\r MH  - Cells, Cultured\r MH  - Child\r MH  - China\r MH  - Crystallography, X-Ray\r MH  - Dystonia/*diagnosis/drug therapy/genetics/pathology\r MH  - Homozygote\r MH  - Humans\r MH  - Male\r MH  - Models, Molecular\r MH  - *Mutation, Missense\r MH  - Oxidoreductases Acting on CH-CH Group Donors/chemistry/*genetics\r MH  - Pedigree\r MH  - Protein Conformation\r MH  - Thioctic Acid/administration &amp; dosage/pharmacology\r MH  - Exome Sequencing/*methods\r OTO - NOTNLM\r OT  - Basal ganglia\r OT  - Dystonia\r OT  - Lipoic acid\r OT  - MECR\r OT  - Mitochondrial fatty acid synthesis\r EDAT- 2021/01/06 06:00\r MHDA- 2021/11/03 06:00\r CRDT- 2021/01/05 20:08\r PHST- 2020/09/12 00:00 [received]\r PHST- 2020/12/23 00:00 [revised]\r PHST- 2020/12/30 00:00 [accepted]\r PHST- 2021/01/06 06:00 [pubmed]\r PHST- 2021/11/03 06:00 [medline]\r PHST- 2021/01/05 20:08 [entrez]\r AID - S1567-7249(20)30245-2 [pii]\r AID - 10.1016/j.mito.2020.12.014 [doi]\r PST - ppublish\r SO  - Mitochondrion. 2021 Mar;57:222-229. doi: 10.1016/j.mito.2020.12.014. Epub 2021 \r       Jan 2.\r \r PMID- 33421601\r OWN - NLM\r STAT- MEDLINE\r DCOM- 20210726\r LR  - 20210726\r IS  - 1872-7573 (Electronic)\r IS  - 0378-8741 (Linking)\r VI  - 270\r DP  - 2021 Apr 24\r TI  - Methanolic extract of Cariniana rubra Gardner ex Miers stem bark negatively \r       regulate the leukocyte migration and TNF-α and up-regulate the annexin-A1 \r       expression.\r PG  - 113778\r LID - S0378-8741(21)00004-0 [pii]\r LID - 10.1016/j.jep.2021.113778 [doi]\r AB  - ETHNOPHARMACOLOGICAL RELEVANCE: Cariniana rubra Gardner ex Miers (Lecythidaceae), \r       is a native and endemic tree in Brazil, whose inner stem bark decoction \r       preparation is used in folk medicine to treat various inflammatory disorders. \r       Previous scientific reports confirmed its popular use as an anti-inflammatory, \r       without, however, evaluating its action mechanisms. AIM: The objective of this \r       study was to determine the cytotoxicity and anti-inflammatory mechanism of action \r       of the methanolic extract of Cariniana rubra (MECr), using experimental models in \r       vivo and in vitro, as well as to identify secondary metabolites present in the \r       extract. MATERIAL AND METHODS: The MECr was prepared by maceration of inner stem \r       bark powder in methanol (1:10 w/v). The in vitro cytotoxicity effect was \r       evaluated in CHO-k1 cells. The Hippocratic screening test was conducted to \r       evaluate the acute toxicity of MECr in mice. The actions of MECr on leukocyte \r       migration, cytokine levels (IL-1β and TNF-α) and annexin-A1 (AnxA1) expression, \r       were carried out on lambda-type carrageenan air pouch inflammation model in Swiss \r       mice. Additionally, the phytochemical analysis of MECr was carried out by \r       thin-layer chromatography (TLC) and spectrometric mass analysis with electrospray \r       ionization ESI(-)/MS and gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS). RESULTS: \r       Treatment of CHO-k1 cells for 24 h with MECr did not cause cytotoxicity \r       (IC(50) &gt; 200 μg/mL), however, the MECr was shown to be cytotoxic after 72 h of \r       cell exposure (IC(50) = 19.90 ± 3.51 μg/mL). In the Hippocratic test, oral \r       treatment of mice with 750, 1500, or 3000 mg/kg of MECr did not show any \r       histopathological changes and mortality during the 14 days of observation. In the \r       carrageenan air pouch inflammation model, MECr reduced (p &lt; 0.001) \r       polymorphonuclear migration (57.7% and 57.8%), leukocyte monocyte migration \r       (74.5% and 61.8%) in the air pouch cavity and in the skin tissue, respectively. \r       MECr also inhibited TNF-α concentration in the air cavity wash (3.2%, p &lt; 0.01) \r       and increased expression of the AnxA1 protein (26.9%, p &lt; 0.01) in the skin \r       tissue, particularly in neutrophils. β-sitosterol (1.95%), gallic acid (1.24%), \r       β-amyrin (0.87%) and stigmasterol (0.66%) were identified as the major \r       constituents in methanolic extract. CONCLUSION: MECr exhibits significant \r       anti-inflammatory action at least by increasing AnxA1 expression and by \r       inhibiting the release of TNF-α pro-inflammatory cytokine and leukocyte \r       migration, which is probably linked to the presence of identified biologically \r       active compounds, especially gallic acid and terpenes. We believe that the \r       results of this study provide a pharmacological basis for the MECr to be \r       considered as a potential therapeutic agent for the treatment of inflammatory \r       diseases.\r CI  - Copyright © 2021 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.\r FAU - Silva, Donata Norman Paulino Brandão\r AU  - Silva DNPB\r AD  - Post-Graduate Course in Health Science, Faculty of Medicine, Federal University \r       of Mato Grosso (UFMT), Cuiabá, Brazil; Department of Pharmacy, University Center \r       of Várzea Grande (UNIVAG), Cuiabá, Brazil.\r FAU - Adriana, Flach\r AU  - Adriana F\r AD  - Department of Chemistry, Federal University of Roraima (UFRR), Boa Vista, Brazil.\r FAU - Martins, Domingos Tabajara de Oliveira\r AU  - Martins DTO\r AD  - Area of Pharmacology, Department of Basic Science in Health, Faculty of Medicine, \r       Federal University of Mato Grosso (UFMT), Cuiabá, Brazil.\r FAU - Borges, Quessi Irias\r AU  - Borges QI\r AD  - Post-Graduate Course in Health Science, Faculty of Medicine, Federal University \r       of Mato Grosso (UFMT), Cuiabá, Brazil.\r FAU - Lindote, Marcus Vitor Nunes\r AU  - Lindote MVN\r AD  - Graduate Course in Medicine, Faculty of Medicine, Federal University of Mato \r       Grosso (UFMT), Cuiabá, Brazil.\r FAU - Zoratti, Marco Túlio Ramalho\r AU  - Zoratti MTR\r AD  - Graduate Course in Medicine, Faculty of Medicine, Federal University of Mato \r       Grosso (UFMT), Cuiabá, Brazil.\r FAU - Oliveira, Ruberlei Godinho de\r AU  - Oliveira RG\r AD  - Area of Pharmacology, Department of Basic Science in Health, Faculty of Medicine, \r       Federal University of Mato Grosso (UFMT), Cuiabá, Brazil.\r FAU - Torquato, Heron Fernandes Vieira\r AU  - Torquato HFV\r AD  - Area of Pharmacology, Department of Basic Science in Health, Faculty of Medicine, \r       Federal University of Mato Grosso (UFMT), Cuiabá, Brazil.\r FAU - Gazoni, Vanessa Fátima\r AU  - Gazoni VF\r AD  - Post-Graduate Course in Health Science, Faculty of Medicine, Federal University \r       of Mato Grosso (UFMT), Cuiabá, Brazil; Area of Pharmacology, Department of Basic \r       Science in Health, Faculty of Medicine, Federal University of Mato Grosso (UFMT), \r       Cuiabá, Brazil.\r FAU - Costa, Luiz Antonio Mendonça Alves da\r AU  - Costa LAMAD\r AD  - Department of Chemistry, Federal University of Roraima (UFRR), Boa Vista, Brazil.\r FAU - Souza, Edineide Cristina Alexandre de\r AU  - Souza ECA\r AD  - Post-Graduate Course in Biodiversidade e Biotecnologia da Amazônia, Federal \r       University of Roraima (UFRR), Boa Vista, Brazil.\r FAU - Silva, Felipe Moura Araújo da\r AU  - Silva FMAD\r AD  - Multidisciplinar Support Center, Federal University of Amazonas (UFMA), Manaus, \r       Brazil.\r FAU - Arunachalam, Karuppusamy\r AU  - Arunachalam K\r AD  - Area of Pharmacology, Department of Basic Science in Health, Faculty of Medicine, \r       Federal University of Mato Grosso (UFMT), Cuiabá, Brazil; Key Laboratory of \r       Economic Plants and Biotechnology and the Yunnan Key Laboratory for Wild Plant \r       Resources, Kunming Institute of Botany, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Kunming, \r       650201, PRChina.\r FAU - Damazo, Amilcar Sabino\r AU  - Damazo AS\r AD  - Area of Histology, Department of Basic Science in Health, Faculty of Medicine, \r       Federal University of Mato Grosso (UFMT), Cuiabá, Brazil. Electronic address: \r       asdamazo@yahoo.com.br.\r LA  - eng\r PT  - Journal Article\r DEP - 20210107\r PL  - Ireland\r TA  - J Ethnopharmacol\r JT  - Journal of ethnopharmacology\r JID - 7903310\r RN  - 0 (Annexin A1)\r RN  - 0 (Anti-Inflammatory Agents)\r RN  - 0 (IL1B protein, mouse)\r RN  - 0 (Interleukin-1beta)\r RN  - 0 (Plant Extracts)\r RN  - 0 (Tnf protein, mouse)\r RN  - 0 (Tumor Necrosis Factor-alpha)\r RN  - 9000-07-1 (Carrageenan)\r RN  - Y4S76JWI15 (Methanol)\r SB  - IM\r MH  - Animals\r MH  - Annexin A1/genetics/metabolism\r MH  - Anti-Inflammatory Agents/analysis/chemistry/*therapeutic use\r MH  - Brazil\r MH  - CHO Cells\r MH  - Carrageenan/toxicity\r MH  - Cell Movement/*drug effects\r MH  - Cell Survival/drug effects\r MH  - Cricetulus\r MH  - Down-Regulation/drug effects\r MH  - Inflammation/chemically induced/drug therapy/metabolism\r MH  - Interleukin-1beta/metabolism\r MH  - Lecythidaceae/*chemistry\r MH  - Leukocytes/cytology/*drug effects/metabolism\r MH  - Male\r MH  - Methanol/chemistry\r MH  - Mice\r MH  - Plant Bark/*chemistry\r MH  - Plant Extracts/an