type="application/opensearchdescription+xml" href="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/persistent/opensearch.xml" title="PubMed search">    <!-- Disables severely broken elements when no JS -->   <noscript>     <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/a447896c-976f-490b-b86b-2668b65114a8/core/no-script.css">   </noscript>         <link rel="canonical" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=%22MECR+mutations%22&amp;format=pubmed" />      </head> <body >       <main class="search-page" id="search-page">     <div class="search-results" id="search-results">                <pre class="search-results-chunk">PMID- 27817865\r OWN - NLM\r STAT- MEDLINE\r DCOM- 20170523\r LR  - 20260518\r IS  - 1537-6605 (Electronic)\r IS  - 0002-9297 (Print)\r IS  - 0002-9297 (Linking)\r VI  - 99\r IP  - 6\r DP  - 2016 Dec 1\r TI  - MECR Mutations Cause Childhood-Onset Dystonia and Optic Atrophy, a Mitochondrial \r       Fatty Acid Synthesis Disorder.\r PG  - 1229-1244\r LID - S0002-9297(16)30434-7 [pii]\r LID - 10.1016/j.ajhg.2016.09.021 [doi]\r AB  - Mitochondrial fatty acid synthesis (mtFAS) is an evolutionarily conserved pathway \r       essential for the function of the respiratory chain and several mitochondrial \r       enzyme complexes. We report here a unique neurometabolic human disorder caused by \r       defective mtFAS. Seven individuals from five unrelated families presented with \r       childhood-onset dystonia, optic atrophy, and basal ganglia signal abnormalities \r       on MRI. All affected individuals were found to harbor recessive mutations in MECR \r       encoding the mitochondrial trans-2-enoyl-coenzyme A-reductase involved in human \r       mtFAS. All six mutations are extremely rare in the general population, segregate \r       with the disease in the families, and are predicted to be deleterious. The \r       nonsense c.855T&gt;G (p.Tyr285(∗)), c.247_250del (p.Asn83Hisfs(∗)4), and splice site \r       c.830+2_830+3insT mutations lead to C-terminal truncation variants of MECR. The \r       missense c.695G&gt;A (p.Gly232Glu), c.854A&gt;G (p.Tyr285Cys), and c.772C&gt;T \r       (p.Arg258Trp) mutations involve conserved amino acid residues, are located within \r       the cofactor binding domain, and are predicted by structural analysis to have a \r       destabilizing effect. Yeast modeling and complementation studies validated the \r       pathogenicity of the MECR mutations. Fibroblast cell lines from affected \r       individuals displayed reduced levels of both MECR and lipoylated proteins as well \r       as defective respiration. These results suggest that mutations in MECR cause a \r       distinct human disorder of the mtFAS pathway. The observation of decreased \r       lipoylation raises the possibility of a potential therapeutic strategy.\r CI  - Copyright Â© 2016 American Society of Human Genetics. Published by Elsevier Inc. \r       All rights reserved.\r FAU - Heimer, Gali\r AU  - Heimer G\r AD  - Pediatric Neurology Unit, Edmond and Lily Children&#x27;s Hospital, Chaim Sheba \r       Medical Center, 52621 Ramat Gan, Israel; The Pinchas Borenstein Talpiot Medical \r       Leadership Program, Chaim Sheba Medical Center, 52621 Ramat Gan, Israel; The \r       Sackler School of Medicine, Tel Aviv University, 69978 Tel Aviv, Israel.\r FAU - Kerätär, Juha M\r AU  - Kerätär JM\r AD  - Faculty of Biochemistry and Molecular Medicine and Biocenter Oulu, University of \r       Oulu, Oulu 90014, Finland.\r FAU - Riley, Lisa G\r AU  - Riley LG\r AD  - Genetic Metabolic Disorders Research Unit, The Children&#x27;s Hospital at Westmead, \r       Sydney, NSW 2145, Australia; Discipline of Paediatrics &amp; Child Health, Sydney \r       Medical School, University of Sydney, Sydney, NSW 2145, Australia.\r FAU - Balasubramaniam, Shanti\r AU  - Balasubramaniam S\r AD  - Western Sydney Genetics Program, The Children&#x27;s Hospital at Westmead, Sydney, NSW \r       2145, Australia; Departme