lements when no JS -->   <noscript>     <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/a447896c-976f-490b-b86b-2668b65114a8/core/no-script.css">   </noscript>         <link rel="canonical" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=MEPAN&amp;format=pubmed" />      </head> <body >       <main class="search-page" id="search-page">     <div class="search-results" id="search-results">                <pre class="search-results-chunk">PMID- 36262091\r OWN - NLM\r STAT- MEDLINE\r DCOM- 20230922\r LR  - 20231003\r IS  - 1744-5094 (Electronic)\r IS  - 1381-6810 (Linking)\r VI  - 44\r IP  - 5\r DP  - 2023 Oct\r TI  - Ophthalmic manifestations of MEPAN syndrome.\r PG  - 469-474\r LID - 10.1080/13816810.2022.2135112 [doi]\r AB  - BACKGROUND: Mitochondrial enoyl CoA reductase protein-associated \r       neurodegeneration (MEPAN) syndrome is an ultra-rare autosomal recessive disorder \r       caused by loss-of-function mutations in the MECR gene. The syndrome is \r       characterized by dystonia in early childhood, basal ganglia signal abnormalities \r       on MRI, and subsequent optic atrophy, with relative sparing of cognition. We \r       characterize the ophthalmic manifestations observed in a patient with MEPAN \r       syndrome, as a detailed account of ocular findings has not been published to \r       date. METHODS: Case study of a patient with genetically confirmed MEPAN syndrome, \r       with full ophthalmic evaluation including slit-lamp exam, sensorimotor exam, \r       fundus photography, retinal ocular coherence tomography (OCT), \r       electroretinography, visual evoked potentials, and visual field testing. RESULTS: \r       The patient exhibited decreased visual acuity of 20/150 in both eyes with \r       moderate dyschromatopsia on pseudoisochromatic plate testing, while peripheral \r       vision was largely intact on Goldmann visual field testing. Fundus exam revealed \r       bilateral optic atrophy with pallor most pronounced temporally, corresponding to \r       OCT findings of diffuse retinal nerve fiber layer thinning most prominent in the \r       papillomacular bundle region and severe ganglion cell layer thinning in the \r       maculae. She also displayed a high frequency horizontal end-gaze nystagmus and \r       symmetric bilateral external ophthalmoplegia. CONCLUSIONS: The pattern of \r       bilateral optic atrophy in our patient with MEPAN syndrome shows predilection for \r       the papillomacular bundle, similar to that seen in other mitochondrial disorders \r       with optic neuropathy, such as Leber Hereditary Optic Neuropathy and Dominant \r       Optic Atrophy. Our patient&#x27;s external ophthalmoplegia is another neuro-ophthalmic \r       finding that may be seen in patients with heritable mitochondrial disease, either \r       as an isolated ocular phenotype or within a constellation of systemic \r       manifestations.\r FAU - Gupta, Priya R\r AU  - Gupta PR\r FAU - Gospe, Sidney M , III\r AU  - Gospe SM , III\r AUID- ORCID: 0000-0003-2838-7983\r AD  - Department of Ophthalmology, Duke University Medical Center, Durham, NC, USA.\r LA  - eng\r PT  - Journal Article\r DEP - 20221019\r PL  - England\r TA  - Ophthalmic Genet\r JT  - Ophthalmic genetics\r JID - 9436057\r SB  - IM\r MH  - Female\r MH  - Humans\r MH  - Child, Preschool\r MH  - Evoked Potentials, Visual\r MH  - *Optic Atrophy, Hereditary, Leber\r MH  - Retina\r MH  - *Optic Nerve Diseases\r MH  - *Optic Atrophy\r MH  - *Mitochondrial Diseases/complications/diagnosis/genetics\r MH  - Tomography, Optical Coherence/methods\r OTO - NOTNLM\r OT  - MECR\r OT  - MEPAN syndrome\r OT  - mitochondrial fatty acid synthesis disorder\r OT  - ophthalmoplegia\r OT  - optic atrophy\r EDAT- 2022/10/21 06:00\r MHDA- 2023/09/22 06:43\r CRDT- 2022/10/20 02:12\r PHST- 2023/09/22 06:43 [medline]\r PHST- 2022/10/21 06:00 [pubmed]\r PHST- 2022/10/20 02:12 [entrez]\r AID - 10.1080/13816810.2022.2135112 [doi]\r PST - ppublish\r SO  - Ophthalmic Genet. 2023 Oct;44(5):469-474. doi: 10.1080/13816810.2022.2135112. \r       Epub 2022 Oct 19.\r \r PMID- 41021813\r OWN - NLM\r STAT- MEDLINE\r DCOM- 20250929\r LR  - 20260307\r IS  - 1091-6490 (Electronic)\r IS  - 0027-8424 (Print)\r IS  - 0027-8424 (Linking)\r VI  - 122\r IP  - 40\r DP  - 2025 Oct 7\r TI  - A mouse model of MEPAN demonstrates a role for mitochondrial fatty acid synthesis \r       in iron-sulfur cluster and supercomplex formation.\r PG  - e2506761122\r LID - 10.1073/pnas.2506761122 [doi]\r LID - e2506761122\r AB  - MEPAN (Mitochondrial Enoyl CoA Reductase Protein-Associated Neurodegeneration) is \r       an early-onset movement disorder characterized by ataxia, dysarthria, and optic \r       atrophy. Here, we report the creation of a mouse model of MEPAN with \r       patient-similar compound heterozygous mutations in the Mecr gene. The MEPAN mouse \r       recapitulates the major hallmarks of MEPAN, including a movement disorder, optic \r       neuropathy, defects in protein lipoylation, and reduced mitochondrial oxidative \r       phosphorylation in the brain. MECR catalyzes the last step in mitochondrial fatty \r       acid synthesis (mtFASII), and the mechanism by which loss of mtFASII leads to \r       neurological disease is unknown. LC-MS/MS-based proteomic analysis of Mecr mutant \r       cerebella identified loss of subunits of complex I of oxidative phosphorylation \r       (OXPHOS) and subunits of the iron-sulfur cluster assembly (ISC) complex. Native \r       gels revealed altered OXPHOS complex and supercomplex formation and changes in \r       binding of the acyl carrier protein (ACP) to mitochondrial complexes. These \r       results demonstrate that MECR plays a key role in the acylation of ACP which is \r       necessary for ACP-LYRM-mediated supercomplex modulation and ISC biogenesis and \r       suggest unique pathways for therapeutics.\r FAU - Murdock, Deborah G\r AU  - Murdock DG\r AUID- ORCID: 0000-0003-0843-2126\r AD  - Center for Mitochondrial and Epigenomic Medicine, Children&#x27;s Hospital of \r       Philadelphia, Philadelphia, PA 19104.\r AD  - Department of Pediatrics, Division of Human Genetics, The Children&#x27;s Hospital of \r       Philadelphia, Perelman School of Medicine, University of Pennsylvania, \r       Philadelphia, PA 19104.\r FAU - Janssen, Kevin A\r AU  - Janssen KA\r AD  - Center for Mitochondrial and Epigenomic Medicine, Children&#x27;s Hospital of \r       Philadelphia, Philadelphia, PA 19104.\r FAU - Keller, Kierstin\r AU  - Keller K\r AD  - Center for Mitochondrial and Epigenomic Medicine, Children&#x27;s Hospital of \r       Philadelphia, Philadelphia, PA 19104.\r FAU - Mitchell, Katherine L\r AU  - Mitchell KL\r AD  - Center for Mitochondrial and Epigenomic Medicine, Children&#x27;s Hospital of \r       Philadelphia, Philadelphia, PA 19104.\r FAU - Beauplan, Maina\r AU  - Beauplan M\r AD  - Center for Mitochondrial and Epigenomic Medicine, Children&#x27;s Hospital of \r       Philadelphia, Philadelphia, PA 19104.\r FAU - O&#x27;Brien, William T\r AU  - O&#x27;Brien WT\r AD  - Department of Pediatrics, Division of Human Genetics, The Children&#x27;s Hospital of \r       Philadelphia, Perelman School of Medicine, University of Pennsylvania, \r       Philadelphia, PA 19104.\r AD  - Neurobehavior Testing Core, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA 19104.\r FAU - D&#x27;Alessandro, Lia\r AU  - D&#x27;Alessandro L\r AD  - Center for Mitochondrial and Epigenomic Medicine, Children&#x27;s Hospital of \r       Philadelphia, Philadelphia, PA 19104.\r FAU - Haltom, Jeffrey A\r AU  - Haltom JA\r AD  - Center for Mitochondrial and Epigenomic Medicine, Children&#x27;s Hospital of \r       Philadelphia, Philadelphia, PA 19104.\r FAU - Wallace, Douglas C\r AU  - Wallace DC\r AUID- ORCID: 0000-0002-7480-8278\r AD  - Center for Mitochondrial and Epigenomic Medicine, Children&#x27;s Hospital of \r       Philadelphia, Philadelphia, PA 19104.\r AD  - Department of Pediatrics, Division of Human Genetics, The Children&#x27;s Hospital of \r       Philadelphia, Perelman School of Medicine, University of Pennsylvania, \r       Philadelphia, PA 19104.\r LA  - eng\r GR  - R01 AG078814/AG/NIA NIH HHS/United States\r GR  - 5R01CA259635/HHS | NIH | National Cancer Institute (NCI)/\r GR  - P50 HD105354/HD/NICHD NIH HHS/United States\r GR  - 5R01AG078814/HHS | NIH | National Institute on Aging (NIA)/\r GR  - U54 HD086984/HD/NICHD NIH HHS/United States\r GR  - no number/United Mitochondrial Disease Foundation (UMDF)/\r GR  - R01 CA259635/CA/NCI NIH HHS/United States\r PT  - Journal Article\r DEP - 20250929\r PL  - United States\r TA  - Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A\r JT  - Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America\r JID - 7505876\r RN  - 0 (Fatty Acids)\r RN  - 0 (Iron-Sulfur Proteins)\r RN  - 0 (Acyl Carrier Protein)\r RN  - EC 1.3.1.38 (trans-2-enoyl-CoA reductase (NADPH))\r RN  - EC 1.3.- (Oxidoreductases Acting on CH-CH Group Donors)\r SB  - IM\r MH  - Animals\r MH  - Mice\r MH  - *Mitochondria/metabolism\r MH  - *Fatty Acids/biosynthesis\r MH  - Disease Models, Animal\r MH  - *Iron-Sulfur Proteins/metabolism\r MH  - Oxidative Phosphorylation\r MH  - Acyl Carrier Protein/metabolism\r MH  - Humans\r MH  - Mutation\r MH  - Oxidoreductases Acting on CH-CH Group Donors\r PMC - PMC12519216\r OTO - NOTNLM\r OT  - genetics\r OT  - iron\r OT  - mitochondrial disease\r OT  - mitochondrial fatty acid synthesis\r OT  - mouse model\r COIS- Competing interests statement:D.C.W. is on the scientific advisory board of Pano \r       Therapeutics, Inc. and Medical Excellence Capital.\r EDAT- 2025/09/29 22:46\r MHDA- 2025/09/29 22:47\r PMCR- 2025/09/29\r CRDT- 2025/09/29 15:13\r PHST- 2025/09/29 22:47 [medline]\r PHST- 2025/09/29 22:46 [pubmed]\r PHST- 2025/09/29 15:13 [entrez]\r PHST- 2025/09/29 00:00 [pmc-release]\r AID - 202506761 [pii]\r AID - 10.1073/pnas.2506761122 [doi]\r PST - ppublish\r SO  - Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2025 Oct 7;122(40):e2506761122. doi: \r       10.1073/pnas.2506761122. Epub 2025 Sep 29.\r \r PMID- 34211152\r OWN - NLM\r STAT- MEDLINE\r DCOM- 20211111\r LR  - 20261010\r IS  - 1530-0366 (Electronic)\r IS  - 1098-3600 (Linking)\r VI  - 23\r IP  - 11\r DP  - 2021 Nov\r TI  - Exome and genome sequencing for pediatric patients with congenital anomalies or \r       intellectual disability: an evidence-based clinical guideline of the American \r       College of Medical Genetics and Genomics (ACMG).\r PG  - 2029-2037\r LID - 10.1038/s41436-021-01242-6 [doi]\r AB  - PURPOSE: To develop an evidence-based clinical practice guideline for the use of \r       exome and genome sequencing (ES/GS) in the care of pediatric patients with one or \r       more congenital anomalies (CA) with onset prior to age 1 year or developmental \r       delay (DD) or intellectual disability (ID) with onset prior to age 18 years. \r       METHODS: The Pediatric Exome/Genome Sequencing Evidence-Based Guideline Work \r       Group (n = 10) used the Grading of Recommendations Assessment, Development and \r       Evaluation (GRADE) evidence to decision (EtD) framework based on the recent \r       American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics (ACMG) systematic review, and \r       an Ontario Health Technology Assessment to develop and present evidence summaries \r       and health-care recommendations. The document underwent extensive internal and \r       external peer review, and public comment, before approval by the ACMG Board of \r       Directors. RESULTS: The literature supports the clinical utility and desirable \r       effects of ES/GS on active and long-term clinical management of patients with \r       CA/DD/ID, and on family-focused and reproductive outcomes with relatively few \r       harms. Compared with standard genetic testing, ES/GS has a higher diagnostic \r       yield and may be more cost-effective when ordered early in the diagnostic \r       evaluation. CONCLUSION: We strongly recommend that ES/GS be considered as a \r       first- or second-tier test for patients with CA/DD/ID.\r CI  - © 2021. The Author(s), under exclusive licence to the American College of Medical \r       Genetics and Genomics.\r FAU - Manickam, Kandamurugu\r AU  - Manickam K\r AD  - Division of Genetic and Genomic Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, Nationwide \r       Children&#x27;s Hospital, Columbus, OH, USA.\r AD  - The Ohio State University College of Medicine, Columbus, OH, USA.\r FAU - McClain, Monica R\r AU  - McClain MR\r AD  - Scientific and Strategic Affairs, Evidera | PPD, Waltham, MA, USA.\r FAU - Demmer, Laurie A\r AU  - Demmer LA\r AD  - Division of Medical Genetics, Department of Pediatrics, Atrium Health&#x27;s Levine \r       Children&#x27;s Hospital, Charlotte, NC, USA.\r FAU - Biswas, Sawona\r AU  - Biswas S\r AD  - Division of Adult Genetics, Department of Pathology, University of California San \r       Francisco, San Francisco, CA, USA.\r FAU - Kearney, Hutton M\r AU  - Kearney HM\r AD  - Division of Laboratory Genetics and Genomics, Department of Laboratory Medicine \r       and Pathology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN, USA.\r FAU - Malinowski, Jennifer\r AU  - Malinowski J\r AD  - Write Inscite, LLC, South Salem, NY, USA.\r FAU - Massingham, Lauren J\r AU  - Massingham LJ\r AD  - Division of Medical Genetics, Department of Pediatrics, Hasbro Children&#x27;s \r       Hospital, Providence, RI, USA.\r AD  - Alpert School of Medicine at Brown University, Providence, RI, USA.\r FAU - Miller, Danny\r AU  - Miller D\r AD  - MEPAN Foundation, Corte Madera, CA, USA.\r FAU - Yu, Timothy W\r AU  - Yu TW\r AD  - Division of Genetics and Genomics, Boston Children&#x27;s Hospital, Boston, MA, USA.\r AD  - Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA.\r FAU - Hisama, Fuki M\r AU  - Hisama FM\r AD  - Division of Medical Genetics, Department of Medicine, University of Washington \r       School of Medicine, Seattle, WA, USA.\r CN  - ACMG 